WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.167 --> 00:00:05.305
- Well, the thing
about this mess is,

00:00:06.172 --> 00:00:08.074
and I bring it up all the time,

00:00:08.074 --> 00:00:10.076
certainly when I'm
talking to Dr. Drew

00:00:10.076 --> 00:00:12.746
who still lives in California,

00:00:12.746 --> 00:00:14.547 align:start size:84% position:16%
it's always, anytime
I talk to anyone

00:00:14.547 --> 00:00:16.549
with a couple of brain
cells rubbed together,

00:00:16.549 --> 00:00:17.784
it's like, they always go,

00:00:17.784 --> 00:00:19.686
"Well, we gotta bottom out,

00:00:19.686 --> 00:00:21.254 align:start size:84% position:16%
and we haven't quite
bottomed out yet,

00:00:21.254 --> 00:00:23.123
but we'll bottom out, and
then when we bottom out,

00:00:23.123 --> 00:00:25.025
then we'll change."

00:00:25.025 --> 00:00:28.728
And I go, "Can't we see
ourselves bottoming out?"

00:00:28.728 --> 00:00:31.865
Like, why do we always have
to completely bottom out?

00:00:31.865 --> 00:00:33.867
You know what I mean?

00:00:33.867 --> 00:00:36.169
It's basically like
saying, you know,

00:00:36.169 --> 00:00:38.038
I got a 15-year-old daughter

00:00:38.038 --> 00:00:41.408
and I had to remove all
the nail polish remover

00:00:41.408 --> 00:00:43.910
from her room 'cause
she was huffing it,

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:46.212 align:start size:84% position:16%
and you go, "You wanna
get her some help?"

00:00:46.212 --> 00:00:48.148
I gotta wait 'til she ODs.

00:00:48.148 --> 00:00:50.483 align:start size:84% position:16%
Like I'll wait 'til
she flat lines,

00:00:50.483 --> 00:00:53.720 align:start size:97% position:3%
and you go, "How about getting
her some counseling now?

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:57.624
She's drinking nail
polish remover."

00:00:57.624 --> 00:00:59.292
"Yeah, but she hasn't flat,

00:00:59.292 --> 00:01:02.028 align:start size:84% position:16%
she hasn't gone into
cardiac arrest."

00:01:02.028 --> 00:01:04.931
It's like, yeah, oh, okay.

00:01:04.931 --> 00:01:08.234
So California is bottoming out,

00:01:08.234 --> 00:01:11.404 align:start size:84% position:16%
but we've not
utterly bottomed out,

00:01:11.404 --> 00:01:15.008
and so we have to wait until
we physically make contact

00:01:15.008 --> 00:01:17.410
with the floor of the basement

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:19.012
and then we'll go,

00:01:19.012 --> 00:01:21.848 align:start position:0%
"You know, I think we gotta
get some new thoughts in here."

00:01:21.848 --> 00:01:24.451
(upbeat music)

00:01:28.855 --> 00:01:30.757 align:start size:84% position:16%
- I'm Dave Rubin,
and as you guys know,

00:01:30.757 --> 00:01:35.495
I am off the grid for August
in an undisclosed location,

00:01:35.495 --> 00:01:38.798 align:start size:94% position:6%
but we went ahead and
pre-taped some incredible,

00:01:38.798 --> 00:01:41.968 align:start size:94% position:6%
fabulous, wonderful,
groundbreaking interviews

00:01:41.968 --> 00:01:45.171 align:start size:84% position:16%
for you in July,
and joining me today

00:01:45.171 --> 00:01:47.640
is the host of the
Adam Carolla Show,

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:49.109
and the author of many books.

00:01:49.109 --> 00:01:50.610
I believe this is his sixth,

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:52.779
but his newest book,
right here in my hand,

00:01:52.779 --> 00:01:54.747
"Everything Reminds
Me of Something:

00:01:54.747 --> 00:01:57.584
Advice, Answers,
But No Apologies."

00:01:57.584 --> 00:02:00.386 align:start size:94% position:6%
Adam Carolla, welcome
back to the Rubin Report.

00:02:00.386 --> 00:02:01.888
- Always good to see you, Dave.

00:02:03.323 --> 00:02:07.093
- Carolla, I have to have a
little mea culpa up top here.

00:02:07.093 --> 00:02:09.596
I'm off the grid right now,
and as I'm interviewing you

00:02:09.596 --> 00:02:12.165 align:start size:84% position:16%
just a few days before
the end of July,

00:02:12.165 --> 00:02:13.600
I have not read the book yet

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:15.702
because I'm gonna
read it in August,

00:02:15.702 --> 00:02:18.605 align:start size:94% position:6%
but is it fair to say this
is your best work ever?

00:02:19.839 --> 00:02:23.042
- No, but it's up there.

00:02:23.042 --> 00:02:23.977
(Dave laughs)

00:02:23.977 --> 00:02:25.211
And you have to understand

00:02:25.211 --> 00:02:28.014
my best work ever is
pretty damn funny.

00:02:28.014 --> 00:02:30.116
This is pretty damn funny, too.

00:02:30.116 --> 00:02:34.454
It's really hard to
kind of rank them.

00:02:34.454 --> 00:02:37.757
People in 50 years,
will all be chicks

00:02:37.757 --> 00:02:39.659
and they're like not
taco bell material,

00:02:39.659 --> 00:02:43.229
but it's hard for me to do it,

00:02:43.229 --> 00:02:47.934 align:start size:84% position:16%
but if you go on
Amazon, it had a 4.8.

00:02:47.934 --> 00:02:52.906 align:start size:88% position:13%
It toggles between a
4.7 star and a 4.8 star.

00:02:53.806 --> 00:02:57.277
My other books are 4.5, 4.6,

00:02:57.277 --> 00:03:02.182
so yeah, it arguably is ranked
a little higher review-wise.

00:03:03.550 --> 00:03:05.285
But it's up there.

00:03:05.285 --> 00:03:06.553
Everything's above a 4.5.

00:03:08.388 --> 00:03:09.856
- I think what you're
really trying to say

00:03:09.856 --> 00:03:12.025 align:start size:94% position:6%
is that your best work
does involve a trampoline.

00:03:12.025 --> 00:03:14.194 align:start size:84% position:16%
Is that what you're
really saying?

00:03:14.194 --> 00:03:15.995 align:start size:84% position:16%
- And it's all in
the rear view mirror.

00:03:15.995 --> 00:03:18.531
(Dave laughing)

00:03:18.531 --> 00:03:21.167
- Speaking of the
rear view mirror,

00:03:21.167 --> 00:03:22.669
I don't see you
in-person anymore

00:03:22.669 --> 00:03:25.572
because I left California
in the rear view mirror.

00:03:25.572 --> 00:03:26.839
You're still there,

00:03:26.839 --> 00:03:28.308
you're on the short
list of sane people.

00:03:28.308 --> 00:03:29.409
How's it going over there?

00:03:29.409 --> 00:03:30.343
You all right?

00:03:30.343 --> 00:03:31.544
Do you need any money or help?

00:03:31.544 --> 00:03:32.912
What can I do for you?

00:03:34.681 --> 00:03:37.250
- It's not good.

00:03:38.785 --> 00:03:42.021
I know people always kind
of talk about the grid,

00:03:42.021 --> 00:03:46.259
and wildfires, and homelessness,

00:03:46.259 --> 00:03:51.264 align:start size:97% position:3%
and drug trafficking, and
street crime, and everything.

00:03:52.432 --> 00:03:55.001
But I will sort of
paint you a picture

00:03:55.001 --> 00:03:58.805 align:start size:84% position:16%
of how LA and
California is doing.

00:03:58.805 --> 00:04:03.710
We tried to open a bridge
in LA like two weeks ago

00:04:04.577 --> 00:04:06.112
and it had to be closed down

00:04:06.112 --> 00:04:09.482
because too much street racing,

00:04:09.482 --> 00:04:13.886
too many people climbing it
and threatening to jump off,

00:04:13.886 --> 00:04:15.722
too much graffiti.

00:04:15.722 --> 00:04:20.627
We literally attempted to open
a bridge in the middle of LA

00:04:21.494 --> 00:04:24.030
to the tune of $600 million,

00:04:25.465 --> 00:04:28.668 align:start size:94% position:6%
and two weeks after it was
opened, it's now closed

00:04:29.836 --> 00:04:33.773
because the people
could not figure out

00:04:33.773 --> 00:04:35.408
a way to walk across it

00:04:35.408 --> 00:04:38.077 align:start size:88% position:13%
without tagging it,
threatening to jump off,

00:04:38.077 --> 00:04:40.613 align:start size:84% position:16%
or sheaving somebody
with a shank.

00:04:41.781 --> 00:04:42.715
That's where LA is.
- So you're saying

00:04:42.715 --> 00:04:44.050
I made the right decision?

00:04:44.050 --> 00:04:45.518
Is that what you're telling me?

00:04:45.518 --> 00:04:46.986
- I think so.

00:04:46.986 --> 00:04:51.090 align:start position:0%
I mean, you have bridges in
Florida and they're mostly open.

00:04:51.090 --> 00:04:53.126
- Seem fine to me.

00:04:53.126 --> 00:04:54.560
From what I can tell,

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:56.229 align:start size:97% position:3%
you're gonna get a gator
under 'em every now and again,

00:04:56.229 --> 00:04:59.666 align:start size:97% position:3%
but they're functioning and
you just go to the other side.

00:04:59.666 --> 00:05:01.634
And it's human there, it's okay.

00:05:01.634 --> 00:05:05.772
- We're talking about putting
speed bumps in this bridge

00:05:05.772 --> 00:05:08.408
and putting a medium
in the middle of it

00:05:08.408 --> 00:05:11.244
because people can't
stop from doing donuts

00:05:11.244 --> 00:05:13.246
in the middle of the bridge.

00:05:13.246 --> 00:05:16.849 align:start size:94% position:6%
- I feel like the nonsense
of LA and California

00:05:16.849 --> 00:05:19.752 align:start size:88% position:13%
must be particularly
hard for a guy like you.

00:05:19.752 --> 00:05:23.289 align:start size:97% position:3%
Because when I think of you
first, you're very functional.

00:05:23.289 --> 00:05:25.124
You like things that work.

00:05:25.124 --> 00:05:28.695 align:start size:97% position:3%
You're a contractor, you
like things that make sense.

00:05:28.695 --> 00:05:31.464
And yet you live in a place
where nothing makes sense.

00:05:31.464 --> 00:05:33.466 align:start size:84% position:16%
Have you thought about
that spiritually?

00:05:35.268 --> 00:05:38.771 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Yeah, if you're
wired the way I'm wired

00:05:38.771 --> 00:05:41.808 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you're very
mechanical and pragmatic,

00:05:41.808 --> 00:05:45.945
it does make no sense to,

00:05:47.380 --> 00:05:52.352
you turn on the TV and the Los
Angeles Commissioner's Board

00:05:53.619 --> 00:05:56.289 align:start size:84% position:16%
or the LA Council
Committee or whatever

00:05:56.289 --> 00:06:00.660
